  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry cold front moves in tonight to early Friday \nmorning, with breezy, cooler and dry weather for Friday. \n\nHigh pressure still remains in control of our weather, supplying \ncontinued sunny and dry weather. Temperatures have warmed back up \ninto the 60s with a few spot 70s popping up, aided by SWly breezes. \nHowever a dry cold frontal boundary is analyzed from central Quebec \nthrough MI as of early Thursday afternoon, and this feature will be \ncrossing through Southern New England overnight into early Friday \nmorning. This frontal feature has limited moisture to work with\nand is moving into an airmass that hasn't seen a drop of rain \nin quite a while. Really could only see some enhanced cloud \ncover overnight tonight from it, with at worst some sprinkles. \nAlthough the past couple nights have been pretty chilly, SW \nwinds around 10 mph for most of the overnight will keep \ntemperatures quite a bit milder than they've been - in the mid \n40s to mid 50s. \n\nCold front should be moving offshore early Friday morning, but with \nit brings strong cold/dry advection and breezy conditions through \nmuch of Friday. With the protracted spell of dry weather and NWly \nwind gusts to around 25 to 30 mph picking up in the late morning \nhours, it could elevate the risk for fire weather given some leaf-\ndrop but the bigger question is that RHs don't look critically low \nat around 40 percent. We'll be coordinating with our state forestry \nand fire weather partners on the state of the fuels but fire weather \nheadlines/SPS could be forthcoming for Friday pending that input. \n925 mb temps tumble to around +2 to +4C by sundown Friday, then \nfalling further into the 0 to -4C range by 12z Saturday. Downsloping \nshould still support highs into the upper 50s to mid 60s (some \nupper 60s SE MA). Although typically gusty conditions ease by \nsundown, a sustained pretty tight pressure gradient continues \ninto Friday evening, which casts some uncertainty on low \ntemperatures. We opted to keep temps from the mid 30s to the \nlow/mid 40s, highest towards the RI/eastern MA coasts where \nwinds will take longer to slacken off, and coolest readings \ninland. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure maintains quiet weather, \npotentially through late next week with warmer than average \nhighs in the 60s by Monday. \n\nA series of high pressures will dominate the weather pattern over \nsouthern New England this weekend clear through much of next week. \nThe post frontal airmass kicks off the weekend much cooler as 925 mb \ntemps drop from 12-15C Friday to 0-3C Saturday. Under dry NW flow \nbeneath a high building in from New York we'll see high temperatures \nclimb just into the mid to upper 50s on Halloween with evening temps \nin the 40s. Fortunately, by then the pressure gradient should have \nlessened enough that winds will be light. Similar forecast for \nSunday but with highs a few degrees warmer, touching the low 60s in \nthe warmest spots like the CT and Merrimack valleys. \n\nA mid-level trough looks to pass well to our northeast around \nTuesday but lack of moisture and the transient nature of the feature \nwill likely keep rain away before another high pressure takes hold \nWednesday into Thursday keeping dry and warmer than average weather \nin place until the pattern may break down moving into next\nweekend.",
